"85_sunit.mst — SUnit-lite, IN THE WORLD (WG9, upstream 6536294 S1).

 It lived in `world/tests/` and was loaded only by `tests/it_world.rs`, which
 made it part of the HARNESS rather than part of the language. That was fine
 while the only tests were ours and the only runner was cargo. It stopped being
 fine the moment the shell grew a Browser that compiles classes into a running
 image: a TestCase someone writes in the Browser has to be a real class in the
 primary, and the Tests tab has to be able to find and run it there.

 So it loads from `world.list` now, for everyone — `macvm run`, `macvm repl`,
 the web GUI, the Windows shell, and the test harness alike. `tests.list` no
 longer carries a copy; two identical definitions would shadow each other and
 hide any drift between them.

 NO EXCEPTIONS IN v1 (SPEC §1.3): error:/DNU kills the whole process, so
 assertion failures are RECORDED via TestResult, not raised — only a genuinely
 erroneous test aborts the run outright. The runner prints each test's name
 before running it, so the culprit is identifiable from stdout even then."

Object subclass: TestResult [
    | runCount failCount failures |
    init [
        runCount := 0.
        failCount := 0.
        failures := OrderedCollection new.
    ]
    ranOne [
        runCount := runCount + 1
    ]
    fail: aString [
        failCount := failCount + 1.
        failures add: aString.
    ]
    runCount [
        ^runCount
    ]
    failCount [
        ^failCount
    ]
    failures [
        ^failures
    ]
    isSuccess [
        ^failCount = 0
    ]
    printOn: s [
        s nextPutAll: runCount printString.
        s nextPutAll: ' run, '.
        s nextPutAll: failCount printString.
        s nextPutAll: ' failed'
    ]
]

Object subclass: TestCase [
    | result current |
    result: r [
        result := r
    ]
    "Hook for subclasses that need per-instance setup; no-op by default."
    init [
    ]
    "ranOne counts ASSERTIONS, not test methods (tests_s06.md's target
     '~215 assertions'/'report prints the assertion count' — the pinned
     pseudocode's `ranOne` placement inside runTest:do: would only count
     ~70 methods across the 15 classes, well under the >= 200 gate).
     Counts on both pass and fail, mirroring 'N run, M failed' where M<=N."
    assert: aBool [
        result ranOne.
        aBool == true ifFalse: [ result fail: current , ': assertion failed' ]
    ]
    assert: aBooleanOrBlock description: aString [
        "SUnit's OWN labelled assertion, and it has to exist here or the
         message resolves to `Object >> assert:description:`
         (80_numeric_completion.mst) — which is a general-purpose assertion
         that SIGNALS. Inside a TestCase that is doubly wrong: the failure
         aborts the whole suite run instead of being recorded and counted,
         and `ranOne` is never called, so the assertions do not appear in the
         `N run` total at all.

         Found the way it always is: fourteen new tests left the run count
         unchanged at 7873, and a single wrong expectation in one of them
         killed all 7873 rather than reporting `1 failed`. Both symptoms, one
         missing method.

         Takes a Boolean or a block, matching Object's, so the two forms stay
         interchangeable for anyone who has already written either."
        | ok |
        ok := aBooleanOrBlock.
        (ok == true or: [ ok == false ]) ifFalse: [ ok := aBooleanOrBlock value ].
        result ranOne.
        ok == true ifFalse: [ result fail: current , ': ' , aString ]
    ]
    assert: a equals: b [
        result ranOne.
        a = b ifFalse: [
            result fail: current , ': expected ' , b printString , ' got ' , a printString ]
    ]
    assert: a equals: b description: aString [
        "WG10. `assert:equals:` reports what it got, which is most of what you
         want; this adds what you were CHECKING, which is the rest. Written the
         moment a Life test wanted to say `a blinker keeps three cells` and had
         to choose between the value and the reason.

         Both, and in that order: the description first because it says what
         broke, the numbers after because they say how."
        result ranOne.
        a = b ifFalse: [
            result fail: current , ': ' , aString
                , ' (expected ' , b printString , ', got ' , a printString , ')' ]
    ]
    deny: aBool [
        self assert: aBool == false
    ]
    runTest: name do: aBlock [
        current := name asString.
        Transcript show: '  ' , current.
        Transcript cr.
        [ aBlock value ] ensure: [ ]
    ]
    TestCase class >> isEnabled ^ <Boolean> [
        "Should this class run IN THIS IMAGE? True unless a subclass says
         otherwise.

         WG9-2, and it exists because discovery found a real hole. The world's
         platform gating lived in `99_run_all.mst` as guards around each
         `TestRunner run:` line — bookkeeping a SCRIPT can see and a DISCOVERY
         cannot. `TestRunner allTestClasses` sweeps the image by superclass
         chain, found `PosixIoTests` on Windows, and ran `Posix close: -1`,
         which took the whole process down: a GUI that vanished when you
         clicked Run Tests.

         So the condition belongs ON THE CLASS, where anything that can find
         the class can also find its answer — a script, a Tests view, a future
         runner nobody has written. The runner script's guards stay as they
         are; they are now a second expression of the same fact rather than the
         only one."
        ^true
    ]

    TestCase class >> runWith: aResult [
        | c |
        c := self new.
        c result: aResult.
        c init.
        c runAll.
        ^c
    ]
]

Object subclass: TestRunner [
    <classVars: Total>
    TestRunner class >> start [
        Total := TestResult new.
        Total init.
    ]
    TestRunner class >> run: aClass [
        Transcript show: aClass name asString; cr.
        aClass runWith: Total.
    ]
    TestRunner class >> report [
        Transcript show: Total printString; cr.
        Total failures do: [:f | Transcript show: f; cr ].
        Smalltalk quit: (Total isSuccess ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ 1 ])
    ]
]
